# What this repository leaves out.
#
# Everything ignored below is produced by building or running the project: a
# reader regenerates it with the same command the book prints. What stays is the
# source, the fixtures the examples read, and the captured output each program
# printed, so a reader can check their own run against the book's.

# ===== generated: build artefacts and local files =====

# Operating system
.DS_Store
._*
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Editors and IDEs
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Local credentials and generated key material. The books that use TLS print the
# keytool/openssl command that makes these, so a reader generates their own.
.env
.env.local
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks

# Scratch, backup and crash files
.scratch/
_scratch/
*_old/
_probe*
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej
*.tmp
*.stackdump

# Maintainers' notes. A capitalised Markdown file other than the three a reader
# expects is a note about the tree, not part of any example.
[A-Z]*.md
!README.md
!LICENSE.md
!CHANGELOG.md

# The example runner's own inputs and records. A reader runs the command the
# book prints; these only tell the runner how, and a raw record holds local paths.
*.args
*.link
*.files
*.raw
outputs/**/_*

# C / C++ build output
*.o
*.obj
*.a
*.lib
*.so
*.dylib
*.dll
*.exe
*.pdb
*.ilk
*.exp

# ===== end generated =====

# ----- this book's own rules, kept -----
# What this repository leaves out.
#
# Everything ignored below is produced by building or running the project: a
# reader regenerates it with the same command the book prints. What stays is the
# source, the fixtures the examples read, and the captured output each program
# printed, so a reader can check their own run against the book's.
